{
  "requirement": "`node` identifies the enclosing outline item. `excerpt` holds the text of the matched byte range, which may be smaller than the enclosing item.\n\n```rust,ignore\n#[non_exhaustive]\npub struct StructuralMatch {\n    pub node: OutlineData,\n    pub excerpt: String,\n}\n\nimpl StructuralMatch {\n    pub fn new(node: OutlineData, excerpt: String) -> Self;\n}\n```",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The reviewed tests adequately check `requirements.sdk.graph.search.StructuralMatch`.\n\n- **Shape and constructor:** `tests/search.rs#fn[callers_can_construct_complete_and_partial_search_results]` uses an external crate to build a `StructuralMatch` with `StructuralMatch::new(address, \"example\")`. It then asserts that `node` equals the supplied `OutlineData` and `excerpt` equals \"example\". This covers the public `node` and `excerpt` fields and the `new` signature. External construction also fits `#[non_exhaustive]`, because a struct literal would not compile there. `struct[StructuralMatch]` in `src/sources/public.rs` does carry the attribute.\n- **`node` is the enclosing outline item:** `tests/source_defaults.rs#fn[built_in_languages_and_structural_search_use_the_project_scope]` runs a real structural search for `call()`. It asserts the node path is `visible.rs` and the selector is `fn[visible]`. It also checks the Python search result's path. `src/sources/tests.rs#fn[structural_search_uses_overridden_parses_their_snapshot_and_requested_scope]` asserts the path `src/one.rs` and selector `fn[cached]` for an overridden provider.\n- **`excerpt` is the matched range, possibly smaller than the item:** Both search tests assert `excerpt == \"call()\"`. The enclosing item is the whole function (`fn[visible]` or `fn[cached]`), so the tests show the excerpt is the matched text and not the item's text.\n- **Public export:** `tests/public_api.rs#fn[public_exports_match_the_appendix]` is a generic export-inventory check. The type and its methods are exercised by the tests above.\n\nThe requirement names no further boundary or failure cases for this type. Result completeness and limits belong to other requirements, and tests for them are present anyway.",
